  "id": "2026-08-23_qknorm-nh1-tax-mechanism",
  "title": "What does QK-norm break at a single head? Decomposing the nh=1 tax into q-side, k-side, and gain geometry",
  "hypothesis": "2026-07-30 found QK-norm is a small but reproducible tax at nh=1 (hd=128): +0.033 bpc over the no-norm baseline, and 2026-08-11 showed the per-token QUERY-magnitude channel cannot fix it (composite inherits the full tax and its alpha disengages to 0.66) \u2014 so the nh=1 casualty is a different quantity than the hd=4 one. Tonight, six arms on byte-identical paired inits at nh=1 decompose the tax into its three remaining suspects: q-side norm vs k-side norm (qnorm_only / knorm_only), learnable full-width gain geometry (qknorm_frozen_gain), and per-token KEY magnitude (qknorm_dynk, the undetached 08-06 machinery on the key path; qknorm_dynqk adds both channels). Predictions: if the tax is key-side magnitude destruction, knorm_only carries most of it, qnorm_only is ~free, and dynk rescues; if it is gain geometry at full width, freezing the gains at 1 removes (or clearly moves) it; if no single arm recovers the baseline's 2.876, the tax is joint and the \"strictly better attention\" claim stays blocked for single-head models.",
